[Music]  it's hard to confuse a jedi master with  a bounty hunter but in 1980 the  distinction wasn't as clear let's go  back in time to see what it was like to  watch the empire strikes back when it  was first released  while the empire strikes back is the  middle chapter of a trilogy many fans  initially felt it left too much to  interpretation in a letter to the  science fiction film magazine star log  one fan named sean bernard wrote i know  they wanted to leave something to settle  in the other sequels but they left a  little too much for instance han solo's  predicament the movie should not have  ended until han was either killed by  boba fett or jabba or rescued by lando  calrissian or chewbacca the former  preferably also the fate of bespin is  not told was it taken by lando's troops  taken by imperial troops or destroyed by  vader this criticism proved common  enough that film star harrison ford  responded to it in an interview with  rolling stone ford said one of series  creator george lucas's real strengths is  not giving you all the information you  need yet at the same time not denying  you anything essential you have a  feeling that you want to know more at  all times i have heard frequently that  there is a certain kind of  disappointment with the ending of the  second film i've heard people say  there's no end to this film or i can't  wait to find out what happens but they  will and that's exactly the effect  intended by the ending  i  am the father  this revelation from the empire strikes  back is often considered one of the most  shocking moments in movie history the  twist was so surprising in fact that  many fans in 1980 doubted what they just  heard star log reader robert l b d  scarola wrote is luke related to vader  most think so now that vader came right  out and said it well i say do you  believe everything you hear vader may  have lied just to enlist luke to his  side vader would then dispose of luke  once he got what he wanted even most of  the cast and crew were kept in the dark  as mark hamill explained on the graham  norton show in 2017 the version they  filmed had an alternate twist in that  scene the way we filmed it vader said  you don't know the truth obi-wan killed  your father  after filming the scene hamill said that  the director irvine kirchner pulled him  aside and told him that darth vader is  actually luke's father however he was  forbidden to tell anyone else including  his castmates that meant that even  harrison ford and carrie fisher didn't  learn the real line until they finally  saw a screening of the film  at the screening when that happened  harrison turned to me and said  hey kid  he didn't tell me that  another line that left fans scratching  their heads after the original  screenings of the empire strikes back  was something yoda said to ben  that boy is our last hope no  there is another  one star log reader named bill smith  theorized could it be vader himself  considering that there was an  equilibrium of power between the good  and dark sides of the force it would not  be impossible to turn vader into the  antithesis of what he is now especially  if luke is truly his son on the other  hand a fan named keith hoffman wrote i  suggest princess leia she is young  enough for the training she withstood  darth vader's tortures she is dedicated  to the cause princess leia not lando  heard luke's cries for help han solo is  not in shape to be going anywhere for a  while and he's too old i wouldn't be  surprised if in the third film leia  instead of luke destroys the emperor as  it turns out each of them was a little  bit right although not in the ways they  expected vader did switch sides at the  end of return of the jedi and leia  ultimately proved to be force sensitive  as well  i know is often considered one of the  most clever improvised lines in movie  history but not everyone felt that way  in 1980 starlog reader jeanette  vogelpole wrote somebody should tell  harrison ford that when a woman  passionately tells a man i love you i  know is not an acceptable response that  scene was not funny it was infuriating  another person who was not a fan of  ford's line was george lucas in a 2011  interview with john favreau the han solo  actor shared how the star wars creator  responded to his improvised line and  george i think that this is a fair  enough to say  he thought it was  horrible  and that it would get a bad laugh  however despite his distaste for ford's  version of the scene lucas allowed the  line to stay in for test screenings  where it was received favorably by  audiences  given how iconic that line is today it  seems like lucas made the right call to  let it remain in the film  while the empire strikes back wasn't  universally beloved upon its release  plenty of fans recognized it as a  masterpiece as soon as they saw it one  positive reaction came from star log  reader david k abernathy who wrote after  seeing the empire strikes back i came  away with the feeling that finally after  all these years science fiction has been  brought to the screen in all its glory  the empire strikes back is so rich so  full of mystery and excitement and most  of all wonder to me the empire strikes  back represents the first true science  fiction film with all of its parts  complete the characters the theme the  special effects and the magic it has the  right to be called the first science  fiction epic another star log reader  arlene berenberg wrote i didn't think  they could do it but they did they made  a movie that was better than the first  star wars more action more dimension to  the characters more and better effects  everything that made star wars so great  the first time plus much more the force  was certainly with george lucas when he  created this fabulous sequel  david gerald a science fiction author  and star trek television writer reviewed  the empire strikes back for star log  number 38 but was not very kind to the  film in his review he wrote just about  every other critic in the country has  been telling you how good the picture is  they've been falling over themselves to  tell you it's embarrassing i feel guilty  for not liking it as much as i'm  supposed to considering that star trek  relies more on grounded science than  star wars gerald's criticisms of the  empire strikes back were mostly about  how it disregarded real world logistics  he was frustrated by the many ways in  which its plot and characters seem to  ignore the physics of the world they  were in  let's go through this shower of  asteroids who cares  and my hair won't get messed up gerald  justified his criticism by reasoning  that the film was using science fiction  devices even if its plot skewed closer  to fantasy he also criticized luke's  growth but enjoyed other parts of the  movie including the musical score  performances production design and  effects  many star log readers were unimpressed  with gerald's review they struck back  heart in issue number 40 where a reader  named michael mallory wrote mr gerald  complains about what he deems  storytelling flaws in logic or science  and the empire strikes back as for the  scientific misrepresentations i can only  say they don't matter this is a fantasy  movie i'm tired of people faulting these  films for not being intellectual enough  who said they were supposed to be  thought provoking few films are perfect  nitpicking is like criticizing an apple  for not being steak when you're hungry  for steak it might be the best apple  you've ever tasted but it's still lousy  steak some letters to gerald were nicer  but overall they weren't the biggest  fans of starlog's official review  like gerald professional critics were  also initially mixed on the empire  strikes back and some of them were  downright savage about it washington  post critic judith martin wrote the  empire strikes back has no plot  structure no character studies let alone  character development no emotional or  philosophical point to make it has no  original vision of the future which is  depicted as a pastiche of other junk  culture formulae such as the western the  costume epic and the world war ii movie  its specialty is special effects or  visual tricks some of which are playful  imaginative and impressive but others of  which have become space movie cliches  frederick s clark of cine fantastique  magazine was also not a fan making a  prediction that really didn't age well  clark wrote the empire strikes back is a  lifeless copy of star wars propelled  chiefly on the momentum of that earlier  film without the likes of a peter  cushing or alec guinness to add some  dignity and solid support mark hamill  carrie fisher and harrison ford flounder  in roles that are certain to doom their  careers regardless of the series success  however as with the fans there were some  critics who loved the empire strikes  back right out of the gate the legendary  gene siskel of the chicago tribune gave  the film three and a half out of four  stars saying his only issues with it  were trivial compared to the strengths  of the film which are considerable and  sometimes even majestic  the empire strikes back was the  cinematic introduction of two characters  who would go on to be immortalized in  merchandise for decades to come yoda and  boba fett however arthur knight a critic  for the hollywood reporter confused them  in his review of the film knight's  column started out smoothly praising the  empire strikes back as the best science  fiction movie sent star wars he also  said that kirschner's direction made the  characters come alive even more than in  the previous film however two paragraphs  in night wrote as to the present episode  despite a couple of phoneded messages  from ben kenobi which collected must  last all of 40 seconds most of luke  skywalker's good advice comes from a  fascinating green hued character named  boba fett and i'm still not certain  whether he's a skillfully animated  puppet or a real live human being  whichever he's one of lucas's strongest  assets in this star wars incarnation  today the archived version of the review  begins with an update correcting the  error but it serves as an amusing  reminder that the icons of the present  were once still new and novel to fans  however even if he mixed up their names  knight was correct in his assumption  that both boba fett and yoda would go on  to count themselves among lucas's  strongest and most marketable assets  if some of the pushback against the  empire strikes back feels strangely  familiar even if you weren't around in  1980 there's a reason for that the  initial response to the empire strikes  back is eerily similar to that of 2017's  star wars episode viii the last jedi  both middle chapters of their respective  trilogies tried to take the star wars  franchise in exciting new directions and  both received mixed reviews from fans  who were initially displeased with the  wide gap between their expectations and  the film itself thanks to the internet  and social media the backlash against  the last jedi was much louder but the  parallels are still unmistakable many  fans slammed the last jedi for its  diversity and assumed political  correctness in addition to critiquing  the coherence of its plot of course few  fans in 1980 were complaining about the  empire strikes back being too  politically correct but there were  similar complaints about the story not  being coherent or compelling  mike ryan of uproxx mentioned in a 2019  interview with the last jedi writer and  director ryan johnson that the empire  strikes back also divided fans when it  was first released the filmmaker  admitted that even he himself was  initially disappointed with empire which  was actually the first star wars movie  he remembered seeing however he  eventually came to a census saying that  after watching and loving return of the  jedi he wound up warming to empire in  fact although he was initially a fan of  the empire strikes back it gradually  became his favorite of the trilogy so  perhaps if the last jedi follows in the  footsteps of its predecessor it may  someday be looked upon as fondly as the  empire strikes back check out one of our  newest videos right here plus even more  looper videos about star wars are coming  soon subscribe to our youtube channel  and hit the bell so you don't miss a  single one  [Music]  you

